@@ -81,6 +81,31 @@ Set `CMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES` to match your GPU:
 
 CMake automatically detects AVX2 / FMA / F16C support at configure time. If supported, `USE_AVX2` is defined and the hand-tuned AVX2 GEMV/GEMM kernels are enabled. On CPUs without AVX2, scalar fallbacks are used.
 
+## PowerPC (AltiVec / VSX)
+
+The PowerPC kernels build for AltiVec (G4, G5, POWER6) and VSX (POWER7 and
+later), 32- or 64-bit, big- or little-endian. CMake picks them from the CPU the
+compiler targets:
+
+- native builds (`FORGE_NATIVE_BUILD=ON`, the default) use `-mcpu=native`;
+- otherwise name the CPU with `FORGE_TUNE_MARCH`, e.g. `-mcpu=7450` (G4),
+  `-mcpu=970` (G5) or `-mcpu=power8`;
+- or choose the kernel set directly with `-DFORGE_CPU_FEATURES=ALTIVEC` or
+  `VSX` (an empty value selects the scalar kernels, e.g. for a G3).
+
+A portable G4/G5 build:
+
+```bash
+cmake -B build -DFORGE_NATIVE_BUILD=OFF -DFORGE_CPU_FEATURES=ALTIVEC -DFORGE_BUILD_PYTHON=OFF
+cmake --build build
+```
+
+Big-endian hosts load ordinary (little-endian) GGUF files: the tensor data is
+converted to host byte order once at load time, in private copy-on-write pages,
+so memory use matches a fully resident model. Big-endian GGUF files load without
+conversion. 32-bit hosts map the whole model file at once, which in practice
+limits models to roughly 1.5-2 GB.
+
